User Identity maps each address on the network to the user logged on
from it, from the logon events of the Active Directory domain
controllers - NXLog CE on each sends them to OPNsense's own syslog-ng,
which takes them from the domain controllers' addresses alone - from
the RADIUS accounting os-freeradius writes, and from the captive
portal's sessions. The latest event wins an address, and the page shows
two sources that clash over one. The directory chosen in System:
Access: Servers says which groups each user is in. The addresses of
each group chosen, and of each user named, are kept in a pf table: an
External alias in Firewall: Aliases for rules to use. A mapping ends at
a RADIUS Stop or the end of a portal session, at the end of its
lifetime, or when another device takes the address. Where os-xgs-npu is
installed, an address that joins a table has its open connections cut.
Off by default.

